    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d145">

                <pb facs="0156" n="144" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg144"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.24/405: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-05-19T18:00:00+03:00">May 19, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1004. The Swedish Minister told me this morning that in a recent conversation
                    with the Counselor of the German Embassy<anchor n="72" xml:id="d145-f1"/> the
                    latter, in referring to discussions taking place in Berlin with the object of
                    increasing Soviet deliveries to Germany had stated that <pb facs="0157" n="145"
                        xml:id="pg145"/> what Germany desired was a substantial increase in
                    deliveries of grain, oils and what he described as “minerals”. He had also
                    remarked that an exchange or barter basis between the Soviet Union and Germany
                    was no longer possible and that the Soviet Government would now have to extend
                    credit to Germany.<anchor n="73" xml:id="d145-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d145-f1" n="72" xml:id="d145-f1-content">
                <p>Gustav Hilger.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d145-f2" n="73" xml:id="d145-f2-content">
                <p>Further details on these economic relations are printed in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Nazi-Soviet Relations, 1939–1941</hi>, pp. 318–346, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">passim</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
